{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel</b> — The priests have no greater involvement in the matter of the festivals than Israelites do; therefore he did not mention Aaron and his sons in this section, but only \"the children of Israel,\" which includes all of them as one, for he does not specify the additional offerings (מוספים) in this section. But he mentioned the festivals here in the Torah of Priests because they are days of offerings, and he alludes to them, as he says, \"and you shall offer an offering made by fire to HaShem,\" and he says at the end of the section, \"These are the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations, to offer an offering made by fire to HaShem — a burnt offering and a meal offering, a sacrifice and drink offerings\" (Leviticus 23:37). But he did not elaborate to explain the additional offerings, because he did not want them to be practiced in the wilderness. And after he enumerated those who would enter the land in the Book of Numbers (חומש הפקודים), and commanded, \"To these the land shall be divided\" (Numbers 26:53), he explained all the additional offerings in the section of Pinchas, to be performed in the land immediately and for all generations. Therefore he said concerning the Day of Atonement, \"And he did as HaShem commanded Moses\" (Leviticus 16:34), for he did so in the wilderness. And in this section he mentioned the lamb of the omer and the lambs of Shavuot (עצרת), for it is well known that they would not be practiced except in the land, since they come on account of the bread, about which he said, \"When you come into the land and you reap its harvest\" etc. (Leviticus 23:10) — but the days themselves take effect immediately.\n\nAnd he mentioned the Sabbath among \"the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations,\" for it too is called a day of mo'ed — a proclamation of holiness. And afterward he distinguished the rest of the festivals from it and said, \"which you shall proclaim in their season (במועדם),\" that is, on whichever day of the week they happen to fall. For the Sabbath is fixed on its day whenever it comes; it does not need to be proclaimed in its season. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Sifra, section 9:5), \"which you shall proclaim in their season\" is a hint to intercalation (עיבורים) — that you shall declare them to be festivals.\n\nAnd the correct interpretation in my eyes is that the meaning of \"the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations — these are My appointed seasons\" refers to those mentioned below in the sections, \"In the first month\" etc., and therefore he repeated there once more, \"These are the appointed seasons of HaShem,\" because he had interrupted with the matter of the Sabbath. Now, he said, \"the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations — these are My appointed seasons\" in the context of sacrificial service, but \"the Sabbath you shall observe to make it a Sabbath of solemn rest\" from all manner of work in the world — for he admonishes concerning the Sabbath many times. And he further alludes here that even when one of the festivals falls, it is not pushed aside from preparing food for the soul (אוכל נפש). And similarly, \"These are the things that HaShem has commanded, that you should do them\" (Exodus 35:1) — concerning the Tabernacle and its vessels, which he mentions in the second section — and he interrupted with the Sabbath, \"Six days shall work be done\" etc. (ibid. v. 2), and then returned and said, \"This is the thing that HaShem has commanded, saying: Take from among you an offering\" (ibid. v. 4), which is the first commandment; because he had interrupted with it, he needed to go back and begin with the first.\n\nAnd a proof for this interpretation: he did not mention concerning the Sabbath, \"and you shall offer an offering made by fire to HaShem,\" as he mentions concerning each and every festival. And he said at the end of the section (Leviticus 23:37–38), \"These are the appointed seasons of HaShem… holy convocations, to offer an offering made by fire to HaShem… besides the Sabbaths of HaShem\" — for the Sabbaths are not included among the appointed seasons of HaShem. And a further proof: he did not say in the second section, \"And HaShem spoke to Moses, saying,\" as he said concerning each and every festival — for the first utterance was the commandment of the festivals, only he mentioned the Sabbath to them to remove from it the legal status of the festivals, not to expound its commandments and laws. Therefore he did not say concerning it, \"and you shall offer an offering made by fire to HaShem,\" as he said concerning the festivals, and he mentioned it at the end (Leviticus 23:38) together with vows and freewill offerings, which were not mentioned here, as I have explained.\n\nThis is the midrash of the Sages who said (Tannaitic Midrash to Leviticus, section 9:7): What has the Sabbath to do with the festivals? etc. — for the Sabbath is not included among the appointed seasons of HaShem at all; the verse merely juxtaposed it to them.\n\nAnd the meaning of \"holy convocations\" (מקראי קדש) is that on that day all of them shall be called and assembled to sanctify it, for it is a commandment upon Israel to gather in the House of Elohim on a festival day to sanctify the day in public, with prayer and praise to El in clean garments, and to make that day a feast, as it is said in the Tradition: \"Go your way, eat rich foods and drink sweet drinks, and send portions to those for whom nothing is prepared, for the day is holy to our Lord; do not be sad, for the joy of HaShem is your strength\" (Nehemiah 8:10). Now, \"holy convocation\" (מקרא קדש) is from the language of \"the called of the assembly\" (קרואי העדה, Numbers 1:16), \"afterward the invited guests shall eat\" (הקרואים, I Samuel 9:13), and likewise, \"upon all the place of Mount Zion and upon its assembly\" (ועל מקראה, Isaiah 4:5) — the places where they are called, where the called of the assembly gather. And Onkelos (on Leviticus 23:2) rendered it from the language of \"who shall call you in the end of days\" (אשר יקרא אתכם, Genesis 49:1) — the language of occurrence (מאורע): on every day that occurs, you shall make them holy. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei, Pinchas 147): Provide for them with food and drink and clean garments. That is to say, their statute shall not be in your hands like the statute of ordinary days, but you shall make for them a proclamation of holiness, to distinguish them with food and clothing from the profane to the holy. And this too is the opinion of Onkelos."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :register {:flags {0 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ת\"כ is resolved as תורת כהנים (Sifra), the Tannaitic midrash to Leviticus; תו\"כ (section 9:7) likewise refers to the same work. חומש הפקודים refers to the Book of Numbers."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:40.153409799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}